    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-210742",
      "knowledge_point_id": "41b7d83f-3b9b-556c-9e49-13df9c3b597e",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}\\cdot  \\\\  & =6\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+C \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})+C",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2{ln}^{3}⁡({x}^{3})+C",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "3{ln}^{3}⁡({x}^{2})+C",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
