      "knowledge_point": "More Complex Cases",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={4}^{x}+2.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}u={4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{4}^{x}+2}\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{4}^{x}+2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x}+2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that ${4}^{x}+2>0$ for all $x.$",
