    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-49674",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d3aaba0a-3b86-58c3-bbc8-22ced09bc8a8",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Definite Integral Using Integration by Parts",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]+\\frac{1}{4}[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[0]+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
